#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qute pre-push branch guard — refuse pushes that land on a guarded branch.

WHY THIS EXISTS AT THIS LAYER
-----------------------------
The plugin's `git-workflow` guard is a Claude `PreToolUse` hook: it reads a
shell command string and infers intent. That makes it useful (it explains the
route *before* the command runs) but structurally leaky in two ways:

  1. it only sees tool calls, so a human at a terminal or a shell script is
     invisible to it;
  2. inferring a push destination from a command line has an unbounded tail of
     shapes — `git push origin HEAD`, unresolvable refspecs, `--repo=origin
     main`, git aliases, `env VAR=x git push …` — each of which was a real
     bypass found by review.

`pre-push` has neither problem. Git runs it after alias expansion, after `env`,
after option parsing — for humans, scripts and agents alike — and hands it the
refs it is actually about to send. There is nothing to parse and nothing to
infer. This is the layer that holds.

WHAT IT IS NOT
--------------
`git push --no-verify` skips every local hook, including this one. That is not
a defect; it is the contract of a client-side hook, and it is what makes this
guard safe to install: it catches the accidental push and yields to the
deliberate one. It is also NOT a substitute for server-side branch protection —
these repos are on a plan where protection is unavailable, which is the reason
any of this exists. If branch protection ever becomes available, it supersedes
this hook rather than complementing it.

Deliberately, this hook does NOT consult the agent-side guard toggle
(`~/.claude/qute-guards.json`). That toggle governs an agent-side Claude hook.
This one fires for a human at a terminal too, and a Claude-session preference
must not silently disarm a git hook for a human. A PERSISTED preference is the
thing being refused here: it disarms every future push, invisibly, including
ones nobody was thinking about when they set it.

`CLAUDE_GUARD_BRANCH_PUSH=0` is the per-invocation override and is honoured. It
is not a weaker `--no-verify`, it is a NARROWER one: `--no-verify` drops every
pre-push hook in the repo — tests, lint, secret scanning — so someone who only
wanted past the branch check has been silently disabling the secret scan too.
An override that costs more than it should does not stop people overriding; it
makes them override too much. This one names exactly what it skips, and skips
only that. The refusal message advertises it first for that reason.

`--no-verify` is a flag, so it is per-push by construction. The variable is
per-push ONLY WHEN WRITTEN INLINE (`CLAUDE_GUARD_BRANCH_PUSH=0 git push …`, or
a child process's own environment, which is how the release tool sets it).
`export`ed — in a shell profile, an `.envrc`, a CI job's env — the variable
disarms this guard for EVERY later push in that environment: exactly the
persisted, invisible disarm refused two paragraphs above, and undetectable from
inside the hook, which sees only a value, never where it came from. Set it
inline; never export it.

There is NO release exemption (qute-plugins ADR-0002). A push is not let
through because its commits look like a release: a subject line is a string
anyone can type, and on a plan with no branch protection a direct push is seen
by no review gate at all.

OPT-IN AND CONFIG
-----------------
Reads the SAME per-repo policy as the `git-workflow` PreToolUse guard, from the
same two surfaces in the same order, so the two layers can never disagree about
which branches are guarded:

  1. `.qute/config.json`, section `"git"` — the consolidated config;
  2. `.claude/git-guard.json` — the legacy flat file, fallback only.

In `.qute/config.json` the fields sit INSIDE the section:

    {
      "git": {
        "protected_branch": "main",       // default "main"
        "integration_branch": "dev",      // default "dev" iff origin/dev exists;
                                          // explicit null = this repo has none
        "release_tool": "commitizen (/ship)"   // guidance text only
      }
    }

In the legacy file the inner object is the whole file. Branch fields written at
the TOP level of `.qute/config.json` are REFUSED, not ignored: read literally
they configure nothing, and "nothing" would mean "not opted in".

The opt-in is a `git` section in `.qute/config.json`, or the PRESENCE of the
legacy file. Neither → this hook is a no-op, which is what keeps it out of
scratch repos and third-party clones. A repo that wants the house defaults
declares `"git": {}`.

INPUT MODES
-----------
1. Native git hook (full fidelity). argv = (remote_name, remote_url); stdin
   carries one `<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>` line per ref.

2. Under the `pre-commit` framework's `pre-push` stage — reduced fidelity, and
   therefore NOT a mode anything installs by default. pre-commit consumes stdin
   itself and re-exposes ONE ref through `PRE_COMMIT_REMOTE_BRANCH` /
   `PRE_COMMIT_LOCAL_BRANCH` / `PRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF` / `PRE_COMMIT_TO_REF`:
     - pre-commit SKIPS ref lines whose local sha is all zeros, i.e. **branch
       deletions never reach any hook** in that world;
     - only the first pushable ref is exposed, so a multi-ref push
       (`git push origin feat/x main`) is judged on `feat/x` alone.
   This mode is supported so it stays legible, not so it can be chosen:
   `install_pre_push_guard.py` ALWAYS installs at the native hook path, moving
   pre-commit's own shim behind the dispatcher rather than running inside it.
   A guard that quietly enforces less than it claims is worse than no guard, so
   the installer's verification FAILS on this mode rather than noting it.

EXIT CODES
----------
  0  allow (also: not opted in, not a branch push, internal error)
  1  refuse

Internal errors fail OPEN with a loud stderr warning. A guard that wedges every
push in the repo the moment it hits an unexpected input is worse than one that
says so and steps aside — but it must SAY so, never fail open silently.

Set QUTE_PRE_PUSH_GUARD_TRACE=1 to emit a `QUTE_PRE_PUSH_GUARD: …` marker line
on stderr for every decision. `install_pre_push_guard.py --verify` uses that
marker to prove the hook is genuinely reachable through git's own hook path
rather than merely present on disk.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

MARKER = "QUTE_PRE_PUSH_GUARD"
ENV_OVERRIDE = "CLAUDE_GUARD_BRANCH_PUSH"
DEFAULT_PROTECTED = "main"
DEFAULT_INTEGRATION = "dev"
BRANCH_PREFIX = "refs/heads/"

# A sha of all zeros is git's "this ref does not exist" sentinel — 40 chars for
# sha1, 64 for sha256.
_ALL_ZEROS = re.compile(r"\A0+\Z")


def _is_zero(sha: str) -> bool:
    return bool(_ALL_ZEROS.match(sha))


def _trace(msg: str) -> None:
    if os.environ.get("QUTE_PRE_PUSH_GUARD_TRACE"):
        print(f"{MARKER}: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def _overridden(env=None) -> bool:
    """True when this push carries the explicit per-invocation override.

    Strict `== "0"`, matching `scripts/guard_config.py:guard_enabled()` — the
    house convention for `CLAUDE_GUARD_<NAME>`. A typo (`=false`, `=off`) is
    therefore NOT an override and the guard still fires, which is the right way
    round: the cost of a misspelling is a refused push you can retry, not a
    silently unguarded one.

    `CLAUDE_SKIP_GUARDS=1` is deliberately NOT honoured. It is a blanket
    Claude-session setting, and this hook fires for humans and scripts too;
    letting a session-wide agent preference disarm a git hook is exactly what
    the module docstring refuses. Only the specifically-named variable counts.
    """
    env = os.environ if env is None else env
    return env.get(ENV_OVERRIDE) == "0"


def _git(cwd: Path, *args: str):
    """Run a read-only git query; return stdout stripped, or None on failure."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["git", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=str(cwd), timeout=10
        )
    except Exception:
        return None
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return out.stdout.strip()


def _repo_root() -> Path | None:
    top = _git(Path.cwd(), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
    return Path(top) if top else None


def _remote_branch_exists(repo_root: Path, branch: str) -> bool:
    return (
        _git(
            repo_root,
            "rev-parse",
            "--verify",
            "--quiet",
            f"refs/remotes/origin/{branch}",
        )
        is not None
    )


class ConfigError(Exception):
    """The repo's git policy exists but cannot be trusted.

    Either surface — the `git` section of `.qute/config.json`, or the legacy
    `.claude/git-guard.json`. Raised, never swallowed. A declared policy is
    somebody stating they want this repo protected — so when it cannot be
    understood, the push STOPS. Failing open here would leave a repo that
    looks guarded and is not, which is strictly worse than no guard at all:
    nobody double-checks a protection they believe is on.

    (Contrast the internal-error handler at the bottom of this file, which
    fails OPEN. The difference is who is wrong. A bug in this script must not
    wedge a repo. A malformed config is a statement of intent that has not been
    met, and the person who wrote it needs to hear about it.)
    """


_SCHEMA_HELP = """
  The git policy lives in the `git` SECTION of .qute/config.json. The fields
  are all optional and all strings:

      {
        "git": {
          "protected_branch":   "main",              // release branch
          "integration_branch": "dev",               // or null if none
          "release_tool":       "commitizen (/ship)" // guidance text
        }
      }

  The `git` wrapper is load-bearing. The same fields at the TOP level of
  .qute/config.json configure nothing, and "configures nothing" would silently
  mean "not opted in" — so that shape is refused, not ignored. The legacy
  .claude/git-guard.json, still read as a fallback, holds the inner object
  unwrapped as the whole file.

  There are two branch slots, not a list. To guard two branches, name them:
  `protected_branch` for the release branch and `integration_branch` for the
  one feature PRs land on first. Those two are the whole model — the agent-side
  guard reads the same section with the same two slots, and a third guarded
  branch in one layer but not the other would be worse than either alone. If
  you need a third, that is a change to both layers, not to this config.

  `"git": {}` gets the house defaults: protect `main`, and `dev` when
  `origin/dev` exists. `"integration_branch": null` means this repo genuinely
  has none. `"$comment"` is accepted anywhere as annotation.
"""


def _describe(value) -> str:
    """A value and its type, for a message someone has to act on."""
    return (
        f"{json.dumps(value) if value is not None else 'null'} ({type(value).__name__})"
    )


def _valid_branch_name(repo_root: Path, name: str) -> bool:
    """Ask git whether `name` could be a branch at all.

    A name git would never produce (`"main "`, `"feat//x"`) can never match a
    pushed ref, so it is silently-unguarded by another route — the same failure
    class as a wrong type, and worth catching in the same place. If
    `check-ref-format` cannot be run at all we accept the name rather than
    block the push on missing tooling; the type check above is the load-bearing
    one.
    """
    out = _git(repo_root, "check-ref-format", f"refs/heads/{name}")
    if out is None:
        # Distinguish "git said no" from "git could not run": re-run and look.
        try:
            probe = subprocess.run(
                ["git", "check-ref-format", "refs/heads/main"],
                capture_output=True,
                cwd=str(repo_root),
                timeout=10,
            )
        except Exception:
            return True  # no usable git — do not block on tooling
        return probe.returncode != 0
    return True


def _normalise_branch(key: str, value: str) -> str:
    """Reduce a configured branch to the SHORT name, or refuse.

    THE INVARIANT: everything in the guarded set is a short branch name, the
    same shape `evaluate()` produces when it strips `refs/heads/` off a pushed
    ref. Normalising here rather than comparing cleverly there is what makes
    that comparison obviously correct instead of correct-by-argument.

    Both spellings of the release branch therefore behave identically —
    `"main"` and `"refs/heads/main"` resolve to `main`. Rejecting the qualified
    form was the alternative; normalising is better because the two spellings
    mean the same thing to a human and a guard that disagrees with the human on
    that point is a guard that quietly protects nothing.

    Anything still under `refs/` after that (`refs/tags/v1`,
    `refs/remotes/origin/main`) is refused: it names something that is not a
    local branch, could never match a pushed branch destination, and would be
    silently unguarded — the exact failure this validation exists to stop.
    `refs/heads/refs/heads/main` is a legal ref name, which is how the earlier
    `check-ref-format` check waved the qualified form through; normalisation
    runs BEFORE that check so what gets validated is what gets compared.
    """
    if value.startswith(BRANCH_PREFIX):
        value = value[len(BRANCH_PREFIX) :]
        if not value:
            raise ConfigError(
                f'"{key}" is just "{BRANCH_PREFIX}" with no branch after it.'
                f"{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
            )
    if value.startswith("refs/"):
        raise ConfigError(
            f'"{key}" is a fully-qualified ref that is not a branch: '
            f"{_describe(value)}. This field takes a branch NAME (e.g. "
            f'"main"); only "{BRANCH_PREFIX}<name>" is accepted in qualified '
            f"form. Anything else could never match a pushed branch and would "
            f"guard nothing.{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )
    return value


def _branch_field(repo_root: Path, raw: dict, key: str, allow_null: bool):
    """One branch field, validated. Returns the name, or None for null/absent."""
    if key not in raw:
        return None
    value = raw[key]
    if value is None:
        if allow_null:
            return None
        raise ConfigError(
            f'"{key}" is null. Remove the field to take the default, or name a '
            f"branch.{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )
    if not isinstance(value, str) or isinstance(value, bool):
        # `isinstance(True, int)` is True in Python, and `True` is a plausible
        # typo for a branch name field, so bool is excluded explicitly.
        #
        # A LIST is refused rather than supported, deliberately. Wanting to
        # guard several branches is reasonable, but this file is read by TWO
        # layers — this hook and the agent-side `git-workflow-guard.py` — and
        # only one of them would honour a list. A third guarded branch that one
        # layer enforces and the other waves through is worse than either layer
        # alone: it is protection you would believe in and only sometimes have.
        # Widening the schema is therefore a change to both layers, made
        # together, not a convenience added here. The message says so, so that
        # someone who wanted two branches learns the supported spelling instead
        # of being told only "no".
        kind = "a list" if isinstance(value, list) else "not a string"
        raise ConfigError(
            f'"{key}" is {kind}: {_describe(value)}. A branch name must be a '
            f"string.{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )
    if not value.strip():
        raise ConfigError(
            f'"{key}" is empty. Name a branch, or remove the field to take the '
            f"default.{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )

    value = _normalise_branch(key, value)

    if not _valid_branch_name(repo_root, value):
        raise ConfigError(
            f'"{key}" is not a valid git branch name: {_describe(value)}. It '
            f"could never match a pushed ref, so it would guard nothing."
            f"{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )
    return value


#: Where the git policy may live, in precedence order. `.qute/config.json`
#: carries it as a `git` SECTION; `.claude/git-guard.json` is the flat
#: pre-consolidation file, still read so no checkout breaks.
#:
#: This hook is installed into `.git/hooks` and runs standalone, so it cannot
#: import `scripts/qute_config.py` the way every other consumer does. It
#: therefore reimplements the two-step lookup. `branch_policy.py` imports THIS
#: function rather than reading the fields itself. The agent-side
#: `git-workflow-guard.py` cannot (it degrades where this refuses) and carries
#: its own copy, which `tests/test_guard_config_conformance.py` holds to the
#: same guarded set.
CONFIG_CANDIDATES = (
    ((".qute", "config.json"), "git"),
    ((".claude", "git-guard.json"), None),
)


class _NotADeclaration(ConfigError):
    """The path is not a regular file — a directory or a broken symlink.

    Distinct from ordinary `ConfigError` because the two mean opposite things
    about INTENT. Malformed JSON, a non-object, a mistyped section: somebody
    WROTE that file meaning it to be config, so falling back to another surface
    would guard whatever the other one says while looking like we obeyed theirs.
    A directory sitting at `.qute/config.json` is a filesystem accident with no
    content and no intent, and refusing every push over it — in a repo whose
    real policy is the legacy file, sitting right there and perfectly valid —
    would be a denial invented by the lookup rather than by the repo.
    """


def _read_config_object(cfg_path: Path):
    """The file's top-level object, with every presence check the guard needs."""
    # `lstat`, not `is_file()`. `is_file()` follows symlinks and answers False
    # for a BROKEN one — so a repo carrying a tracked config that points at a
    # gitignored or machine-specific path reads as opted in to anyone looking at
    # `git ls-files`, while the guard silently treated it as "no config" and
    # waved every push through. Same for a directory of that name.
    st = os.lstat(cfg_path)
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
            try:
                target = os.readlink(cfg_path)
            except OSError:
                target = "?"
            what = f"a symlink (-> {target})"
        elif stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
            what = "a directory"
        else:
            what = "not a regular file"
        raise _NotADeclaration(
            f"{cfg_path} is {what}. The guard config must be a regular file — "
            "the repo looks opted in, so refusing rather than quietly guarding "
            f"nothing.{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )
    try:
        text = cfg_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as exc:
        raise ConfigError(f"{cfg_path} exists but could not be read: {exc}") from exc
    try:
        raw = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError as exc:
        raise ConfigError(f"{cfg_path} is not valid JSON: {exc}{_SCHEMA_HELP}") from exc
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        raise ConfigError(
            f"{cfg_path} must contain a JSON object, got {_describe(raw)}."
            f"{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )
    return raw


def load_config(repo_root: Path):
    """Resolved guard config, or None when the repo is not opted in.

    Opt-in is an explicit `git` section in `.qute/config.json`, or the presence
    of the legacy `.claude/git-guard.json`. Neither → None, a total no-op.
    Present but malformed → ConfigError, which refuses the push.

    Keeps the same fields, defaults and CANDIDATE ORDER as
    `hooks/git-workflow-guard.py`. That layer does not import this function —
    their failure behaviour differs on purpose (this one refuses the push, that
    one degrades loudly to the house defaults) — so what must not diverge is
    which surfaces are consulted, in which order, and what counts as an opt-in.
    `branch_policy.py` DOES import this function, and is the reason the parser
    for the fields themselves lives here and only here.
    """
    cfg_path = None
    raw = None
    for rel, key in CONFIG_CANDIDATES:
        candidate = repo_root.joinpath(*rel)
        try:
            data = _read_config_object(candidate)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        except NotADirectoryError:
            # A parent component is a regular file, not a directory.
            #
            # For the CONSOLIDATED candidate that is benign: `.qute` being a
            # file only means nothing is declared there, so move on.
            #
            # For the LEGACY candidate it is not. It is the last surface, so
            # skipping it returns None — "not opted in" — and every push to a
            # guarded branch exits 0. That is the "looks guarded, isn't" state
            # `ConfigError` exists for, so it refuses like any unreadable config.
            if key is not None:
                continue
            raise ConfigError(
                f"{candidate} cannot be read: a parent path component is a "
                "file, not a directory."
            ) from None
        except OSError as exc:
            raise ConfigError(
                f"{candidate} could not be examined: {exc}"
            ) from exc
        except _NotADeclaration:
            # No content, so no intent — fall through to a surface that has
            # some. Only for the consolidated candidate, and only when a legacy
            # file actually exists to answer; otherwise this is the repo's one
            # config and being unreadable must still refuse.
            legacy_rel = CONFIG_CANDIDATES[1][0]
            if key is not None and repo_root.joinpath(*legacy_rel).exists():
                print(
                    f"{MARKER}: {candidate} is not a regular file — falling back "
                    f"to {'/'.join(legacy_rel)}. Fix or remove it.",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                continue
            raise
        if key is not None:
            # Branch fields at the TOP level of the consolidated file are a
            # mistake in every arrangement, so this runs BEFORE the section
            # lookup rather than only when the section is missing:
            #
            #   * no `git` section — the legacy flat object was pasted in and
            #     the wrapper forgotten. Falling through reads as "not opted
            #     in": every push waved past, on a file whose author plainly
            #     meant to configure this guard.
            #   * a `git` section as well — the top-level copy is read by
            #     NOTHING, so a branch named only up there is silently
            #     unguarded while the author believes it is protected.
            #   * a valid legacy file too — falling back to it would enforce a
            #     policy the repo's newest statement does not describe.
            #
            # BRANCH fields only. `release_tool` is guidance text with no
            # security meaning; refusing every push over a stray string costs
            # more than it protects.
            misplaced = sorted(set(data) & {"protected_branch", "integration_branch"})
            if misplaced:
                where = (
                    f"a '{key}' section as well, which is the one READ — the "
                    "top-level copy configures nothing. Keep one copy, inside"
                    if key in data
                    else f"no '{key}' section. They belong inside"
                )
                raise ConfigError(
                    f"{candidate} has {', '.join(misplaced)} at the TOP level "
                    f'but {where} "{key}": {{ … }}.{_SCHEMA_HELP}'
                )
            if key not in data:
                # The consolidated file exists but says nothing about git.
                # A file's existence is not itself an opt-in — fall through.
                continue
            section = data[key]
            if not isinstance(section, dict):
                raise ConfigError(
                    f"{candidate}: section '{key}' must be a JSON object, "
                    f"got {_describe(section)}.{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
                )
            data = section
        cfg_path, raw = candidate, data
        break

    if raw is None:
        return None

    # `$comment` is the annotation key `.qute/config.json` uses throughout. It is
    # documentation, not policy, so it is a RECOGNISED no-op — otherwise a
    # comment somebody wrote on purpose prints a warning on every push.
    unknown = sorted(
        set(raw)
        - {"protected_branch", "integration_branch", "release_tool", "$comment"}
    )
    if unknown:
        # Warn, never refuse: the agent-side guard reads this same file and may
        # grow a field first. Hard-failing on a key we do not know yet would
        # let one layer's release break the other.
        print(
            f"{MARKER}: {cfg_path} has unrecognised field(s) "
            f"{', '.join(unknown)} — ignored",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )

    protected = _branch_field(repo_root, raw, "protected_branch", allow_null=False)
    if protected is None:
        protected = DEFAULT_PROTECTED

    if "integration_branch" in raw:
        # Explicit null means "this repo genuinely has no integration branch" —
        # the default must not resurrect one.
        integration = _branch_field(
            repo_root, raw, "integration_branch", allow_null=True
        )
    else:
        integration = (
            DEFAULT_INTEGRATION
            if _remote_branch_exists(repo_root, DEFAULT_INTEGRATION)
            else None
        )
    if integration == protected:
        integration = None

    release_tool = raw.get("release_tool")
    if release_tool is not None and not isinstance(release_tool, str):
        raise ConfigError(
            f'"release_tool" must be a string, got {_describe(release_tool)}.'
            f"{_SCHEMA_HELP}"
        )

    return {
        "protected": protected,
        "integration": integration,
        "release_tool": release_tool,
        # The file that answered, so a refusal names the file to go and edit
        # rather than one of the candidates that may not exist.
        "source_path": str(cfg_path.relative_to(repo_root)),
    }


def parse_ref_lines(text: str):
    """Parse git's pre-push stdin into 4-tuples.

    Each line is `<local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>`. Git ref
    names cannot contain whitespace, and a deletion sends the literal
    `(delete)` as the local ref — so a plain whitespace split is exact. Lines
    that are not four fields are ignored rather than guessed at.
    """
    refs = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) != 4:
            continue
        refs.append(tuple(parts))
    return refs


def refs_from_pre_commit_env(env=None):
    """Reconstruct a single ref line from the pre-commit framework's env vars.

    Returns [] when this is not a pre-commit `pre-push` invocation.
    """
    env = os.environ if env is None else env
    remote_ref = env.get("PRE_COMMIT_REMOTE_BRANCH")
    if not remote_ref:
        return []
    local_ref = env.get("PRE_COMMIT_LOCAL_BRANCH") or "HEAD"
    local_sha = env.get("PRE_COMMIT_TO_REF") or "0" * 40
    remote_sha = env.get("PRE_COMMIT_FROM_REF") or "0" * 40
    return [(local_ref, local_sha, remote_ref, remote_sha)]


def collect_refs(stdin_text: str, env=None):
    """The refs being pushed, from stdin when available, else pre-commit env."""
    refs = parse_ref_lines(stdin_text)
    if refs:
        return refs, "stdin"
    env_refs = refs_from_pre_commit_env(env)
    if env_refs:
        return env_refs, "pre-commit-env"
    return [], "none"


def evaluate(refs, guarded):
    """First guarded destination in `refs`, as (branch, kind), else None.

    `kind` is "delete" when the push would remove the branch on the remote,
    "create" when it would create it, else "update".
    """
    for local_ref, local_sha, remote_ref, remote_sha in refs:
        if not remote_ref.startswith(BRANCH_PREFIX):
            # Tags, notes, refs/pull/* — not a branch, not our business.
            continue
        branch = remote_ref[len(BRANCH_PREFIX) :]
        if branch not in guarded:
            continue
        if _is_zero(local_sha):
            kind = "delete"
        elif _is_zero(remote_sha):
            kind = "create"
        else:
            kind = "update"
        return branch, kind
    return None


def refusal_message(branch, kind, remote_name, cfg, source) -> str:
    guarded = ", ".join(b for b in (cfg["protected"], cfg["integration"]) if b)
    verb = {
        "delete": f"delete '{branch}' on {remote_name}",
        "create": f"create '{branch}' on {remote_name}",
        "update": f"push directly to '{branch}' on {remote_name}",
    }[kind]
    lines = [
        "",
        f"pre-push: REFUSED — this push would {verb}.",
        "",
        f"  '{branch}' is a guarded branch in this repo. Guarded work reaches it",
        "  through a pull request, because that is where review and CI run. A",
        "  direct push runs neither.",
        "",
        "  Do this instead:",
        "",
        "    git switch -c <type>/<slug>        # feat/… fix/… docs/… chore/…",
        "    git push -u origin <type>/<slug>",
        # Base the PR on the branch they were actually trying to reach, NOT
        # always `protected`. Under the documented model feature work lands on
        # the integration branch first, so a refusal for `dev` that says
        # "--base main" sends the change to the wrong place — and a refusal
        # whose instructions are wrong is worse than a bare refusal, because it
        # gets followed.
        # shlex.quote: `branch` comes from a config file the REPOSITORY ships,
        # and git's ref rules permit `$`, `&`, `|`, `(`, backticks — verified,
        # `main$(id)` is a legal branch name. This line is guidance a human is
        # meant to copy and paste, so an unquoted name would be a
        # copy-pasteable command injection. (A refusal that hands you a
        # poisoned command is a special kind of bad refusal.)
        f"    gh pr create --base {shlex.quote(branch)}",
        "",
        f"  Guarded branches ({guarded}) come from "
        f"{cfg.get('source_path') or 'this repo’s git policy'}.",
    ]
    if cfg.get("release_tool"):
        lines.append(f"  Releases in this repo go through: {cfg['release_tool']}.")
    if kind == "delete":
        lines += [
            "",
            "  Deleting a guarded branch is almost never what you meant. If it is,",
            "  do it deliberately in the remote's UI where it is auditable.",
        ]
    lines += [
        "",
        "  This is a local hook, so it yields to intent. To override on purpose:",
        "",
        f"    {ENV_OVERRIDE}=0 git push …    # skips THIS check only",
        "    git push --no-verify …               # skips every pre-push hook",
        "",
        "  Prefer the first. --no-verify also drops the secret scan, the tests and",
        "  the lint hook, which is more than you meant if you only wanted this one.",
        "",
    ]
    if source == "pre-commit-env":
        lines += [
            "  (running under the pre-commit framework — see install_pre_push_guard.py",
            "   for why the native hook path sees strictly more than this one does)",
            "",
        ]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def main(argv, stdin_text: str) -> int:
    remote_name = argv[0] if argv else "origin"

    repo_root = _repo_root()
    if repo_root is None:
        _trace("allow: not a git repo")
        return 0

    # Checked BEFORE load_config, so the override also releases a push wedged by
    # an unusable config. That path fails closed on purpose (see ConfigError),
    # and the escape from it should not have to be `--no-verify` — which would
    # drop the secret scan as the price of a JSON typo.
    if _overridden():
        _trace("allow: overridden via " + ENV_OVERRIDE)
        # Loud, unconditionally. This file's stated rule is that a guard which
        # does not run must SAY so; an override is that case, chosen rather than
        # stumbled into, and the line is what makes it visible in a CI log.
        print(
            f"{MARKER}: branch guard SKIPPED for this push "
            f"({ENV_OVERRIDE}=0). Other pre-push hooks still ran.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 0

    try:
        cfg = load_config(repo_root)
    except ConfigError as exc:
        # Fail CLOSED. Someone committed this file to get protection; a config
        # we cannot read is that intention unmet, not permission to proceed.
        _trace("refuse: unusable config")
        print(
            f"\npre-push: REFUSED — this repo's branch-guard config is unusable, "
            f"so the guard cannot tell which branches to protect.\n\n  {exc}\n"
            "  Fix the file and push again. To override deliberately:\n\n"
            f"    {ENV_OVERRIDE}=0 git push …    # skips THIS check only\n"
            "    git push --no-verify …               # skips every pre-push hook\n",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    if cfg is None:
        _trace(
            "allow: repo not opted in (no git section in .qute/config.json, "
            "no .claude/git-guard.json)"
        )
        return 0

    refs, source = collect_refs(stdin_text)
    if not refs:
        _trace(f"allow: no ref lines (source={source})")
        return 0

    guarded = {b for b in (cfg["protected"], cfg["integration"]) if b}
    hit = evaluate(refs, guarded)
    if hit is None:
        _trace(f"allow: no guarded destination (source={source})")
        return 0

    branch, kind = hit

    # No release exemption (qute-plugins ADR-0002). A push is not allowed
    # because its commits LOOK like a release: the subject is a string anyone
    # can type, a path allowlist cannot tell a version bump from a
    # dependency-pin change, and on a plan with no branch protection a direct
    # push meets no review gate. The 10.5.x exemption also passed force-pushes
    # and `any()`-corroborated commits. The release tool declares its push
    # with `ENV_OVERRIDE`, checked above.
    _trace(f"refuse {branch} kind={kind} source={source}")
    print(refusal_message(branch, kind, remote_name, cfg, source), file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        _stdin = "" if sys.stdin is None or sys.stdin.isatty() else sys.stdin.read()
    except Exception:
        _stdin = ""
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:], _stdin))
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as exc:  # fail open, but loudly — never silently
        print(
            f"{MARKER}: internal error ({exc!r}) — branch guard did NOT run for "
            "this push. Please report this; the push is being allowed.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        sys.exit(0)
